content.text:
I've set up a Rust library crate in `/work` called `platkit`, which is a placeholder name you can change in `Cargo.toml`. Formatting, docs, clippy (with warnings treated as errors) and tests all pass, and the crate compiles on every target I tried.

**How the conditional compilation works:** all platform selection uses `cfg_select!`, which is built into the standard library. I checked that it is stable from Rust 1.95 and not on 1.94, so the minimum Rust version is 1.95 and you don't need the `cfg-if` crate. It reads like a `match`: the first matching branch is used, and the final `_` branch catches everything else, so exactly one branch gets compiled. It replaces pairs of `#[cfg(x)]` / `#[cfg(not(x))]` attributes:

```rust
cfg_select! {
    unix => { mod unix; pub use unix::*; }
    windows => { mod windows; pub use windows::*; }
    target_family = "wasm" => { mod wasm; pub use wasm::*; }
    _ => { mod fallback; pub use fallback::*; }
}
```

